[s0l0!-header][h1]Scope[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Scope is where a variable can be used.
There are two main scopes in Go: [b]local[/b] and [b]global[/b].

A variable defined in the function is called a [b]local[/b] variable. Their scope is only in the function body, which means they only exist within their function.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="go" ]func test() {
  var x = 8
  fmt.Println(x)
}
 
[/code]
Now, [b]x[/b] is a local variable and is available only in the body of the [b]test[/b]() function.

Trying to access it in another function, for example [b]main[/b](), will cause an error:[code format="go" codeId="3328"]func test() {
  var x = 8
  fmt.Println(x)
}
 func main() {
  fmt.Println(x)
}[/code]
[note]Function arguments are also local variables for that function.[/note]

