[s0l0!-header][h1]Scope[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A variable defined outside the local scope is called a [b]global[/b] variable. Global variables can be used throughout the package.

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="go" codeId="3329"]var x = 8

func test() {
  fmt.Println(x)
}
func main() {
  fmt.Println(x)
} [/code]
The variable [b]x[/b] is declared outside of the functions, making it a global variable, which is accessible anywhere in the package.
[note]Global variables are often considered a bad practice.
It is better to pass variables as function arguments.[/note]

